Subject: [PATCH 19/19] amd64: preopen never closed /chosen, so the prompt was never clean

One word, and the arch it brings amd64 into line with is x86.

arch/x86/init.fs:52 ends preopen with `device-end`. arch/amd64/init.fs's copy
never did -- so the LAST preopen run by the SYSTEM-initializer left /chosen as
the active package, for the rest of the boot and all the way to the prompt.
Measured before this patch:

                        amd64            x86
  active-package u.     13d708           0
  pwd                   /chosen          no active device
  get-order .           2                1

WHY THAT IS NOT COSMETIC. active-package! (forth/device/package.fs) does

    ?dup if
      forth-wordlist over >dn.methods @ 2 set-order
      >dn.methods @ set-current
    else
      forth-wordlist dup 1 set-order set-current
    then

-- with a package active, CURRENT is the node's method list. That is correct
IEEE 1275 and is exactly how `: open ... ;` inside new-device/finish-device
becomes a node method; there is nothing to fix in $create, value or variable.
The consequence when nobody closes the node is that EVERY definition made at the
amd64 prompt was silently a method of /chosen:

    0 > variable la  5 la !  la @ .    5  ok
    0 > device-end  la @ .             la: undefined word.

Defined, usable, and then gone -- because device-end sets the search order back
to [forth-wordlist] alone, and the word was never in it. Anything that walks the
device tree between a definition and its use does this, which is why it surfaced
first inside a multi-line Forth probe and was misread as a property of
`evaluate`. It is not: the two lines above were typed straight at the prompt.

This is the SECOND of two unbalanced sites in this file. Line 9's
`" /" find-device` is the other, fixed by patch 18. Each arch got a different
subset of the two right, which is why one defect kept presenting as four
unrelated symptoms:

  arch      line-9 find-device closed   preopen closes   prompt
  x86       no  (patch 18)              YES (:52)        clean
  amd64     no  (patch 18)              no  (THIS)       /chosen active
  sparc64   yes (device-end :51)        no               UNVERIFIED
  sparc32   yes (device-end :38)        no               UNVERIFIED
  ppc       never opens one             n/a              UNVERIFIED

NOT FIXED HERE: arch/sparc64/init.fs and arch/sparc32/init.fs have the identical
omission. This lab cannot boot sparc, so they are recorded as an UNKNOWN rather
than patched blind -- tools/check-patch-scope.sh would demand an `Arch-tested:`
line naming three arches, and this lab cannot honestly write one for a sparc
file.

After, amd64 is byte-for-byte x86's answer: active-package 0, "no active
device", order 1. The assertion added to the `amd64` smoke track is the OUTCOME
-- a variable defined at the prompt survives device-end -- not `active-package
u.`, which is the mechanism. The same probe was added to the `multiboot` track
as its CONTROL: x86 has always passed it, which is what distinguishes "the fix
works" from "the probe cannot fail".

Negative control: this device-end deleted and amd64 rebuilt, the amd64 track
fires its REGRESSION line and the multiboot track stays green.

All twelve smoke tracks pass on x86, amd64 and ppc.

--- a/arch/amd64/init.fs
+++ b/arch/amd64/init.fs
@@ -49,6 +49,33 @@
   else
     2drop
   then
+  \ arch/x86/init.fs:52 has this line and amd64's copy never did, so the LAST
+  \ preopen of the SYSTEM-initializer left /chosen active -- for the rest of the
+  \ boot and all the way to the prompt. Measured before this line existed:
+  \
+  \     0 > active-package u.   13d708
+  \     0 > pwd                 /chosen        ( x86: "no active device" )
+  \     0 > get-order .         2              ( x86: 1 )
+  \
+  \ active-package! (forth/device/package.fs) sets CURRENT to the active node's
+  \ method list, which is correct IEEE 1275 -- it is how `: open ... ;` inside
+  \ new-device becomes a node method. The consequence when nobody closes the
+  \ node is that EVERY definition made at the amd64 prompt landed in /chosen
+  \ and evaporated the moment anything called device-end:
+  \
+  \     0 > variable la  5 la !  la @ .    5  ok
+  \     0 > device-end  la @ .             la: undefined word.
+  \
+  \ Same shape as patches 14, 16 and 18, and the second of the two unbalanced
+  \ sites in this file -- line 9's `" /" find-device` is the other one. Each
+  \ arch got a different subset of the two right, which is why one defect kept
+  \ presenting as four unrelated symptoms.
+  \
+  \ NOT FIXED HERE: arch/sparc64/init.fs has the identical omission. This lab
+  \ cannot boot sparc, so it is recorded as an UNKNOWN rather than patched
+  \ blind -- see tools/check-patch-scope.sh, which would demand an Arch-tested:
+  \ line this lab cannot honestly write.
+  device-end
